le are several lines branching out. In the top left of the rectangle, CH2 CH2 binds up and left to O, which pinds up and left to P (in green circle). On the top right of the rectangle, CH2 binds up and right to O, which binds up and right to P (in green circle). In the middle of these two rectangles 2H20 has a curved (to the left) arrow to 2Pi. To the right of 2Pi, 2ATP has a curved arrow (to the right) to 2ADP. Outside of those two arrows, on the left, is an arrow pointing upward, touching the arch of the left arrow, identified as PP1 (in blue). An arrow on the right of the right curved arrow points down and touches the arch of that curved arrow. It is identified as phosphorylase b kinase (in blue). Above that is a green rectangle in a circle that has a dashed arrow pointing to it identified as glucagon (liver). Below is another green rectangle in a circle, which has a dashed arrow pointing to it, identified as epinephrine, [up arrow][Ca2+], [up arrow][AMP] (muscle). " id="518" name="Google Shape;518;p68"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="0" l="0" r="0" t="0"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="3194050" y="2903537"/><a:ext cx="2901950" cy="3878262"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic></p:spTree></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:sld>